<mjml lang="en">
  <mj-head>
    <!--
      PROBE — Video overlay: WATCH Button / Play Icon / None (2026-09-29).
      Follows importer 451aaad (data-alt-member) and TPL 637f573, which gave
      Video Block and Video Feature one Display Select for the overlay and
      held the band's height with a 128px mj-raw row in each 25px rail
      (video-band rescaled 144/175/275 -> 105/136/236). Chromium measured
      every option at 338px (600 and 375 wide), overlay centred. No real
      client has been measured.

      Not a catalog page. First upload assets/icon-play-circle_orange.png to
      EN folder 20002 (the live copy points at the CDN; without it B2 is a
      broken image). Build, paste dist/probe_video-overlay-2026-09-29_live.html
      into an EN email, send to EoA. Read the DELIVERED HTML first
      (display/email_html/<TEST_ID>), then the Word-engine renders (Outlook
      2016 and 2019 at 120 DPI, Outlook 2021 Win11, M365 Win11), then Apple
      Mail / iOS / Gmail web / Gmail Android / Outlook.com. Light and dark
      both; dark is recorded, not pass/fail.

      The three options are authored RESOLVED (what each Select value sends),
      not with data-alt-member: the build would drop the alternate. The head
      is src/main.mjml's, verbatim except the styles.css path.

      == CLAIMS
        C1  Every option renders the SAME band height in every client:
            338px at 600 wide (x1.25 at 120 DPI).
        C2  The overlay is centred in the band, both axes, in every client:
            the rails' vertical-align="middle" reaches Word as valign.
        C3  The 128px rail rows hold 128px in the Word engine (no Word
            line-height or empty-row inflation), and EN delivers them
            intact: <td height="128" style="...height:128px;line-height:128px">.
        C4  The icon renders at 128px (not its 256px native size) and sharp,
            including Outlook desktop.
        C5  On phones the band keeps its height and the overlay stays centred
            (mj-group never stacks, so the rails stay beside the slot).
        C6  (baseline, recorded, not pass/fail) the OLD shape's band in each
            client, with the button and without it: how big the drift was.

      == R — THE REAL BAND, kayak photo. Eyeball test.
        R1 WATCH        option 1, as shipped.
        R2 PLAY ICON    option 2 resolved.
        R3 NONE         option 3 resolved: the column holds only the comment.
        R4 OLD          pre-637f573: 144px padding, bare rails, button.
        R5 OLD NONE     R4 with the button excluded (the 50px drop).
        PASS: R1 = R2 = R3 in height in each client; R4 is the reference
        height the old math was built for; R5 shows the drift.

      == M — METERED TWINS. Same group geometry on a flat ground, palette
         paint, so heights are countable:
           SUN    #F7931E  the 128px ruler at the top of the series
           GRASS  #8CC63F  the band ground (105px padding top and bottom)
           SKY    #5DD8D8  the rail rows; Sky height IS the slot
        Band px = (Grass band pixels / Sun ruler pixels) x 128. Holds at any DPI.
        M1 WATCH, M2 PLAY ICON, M3 NONE.  Expect band 338, Sky 128, everywhere.
        Reading: Sky = 128 but band != 338 -> the section padding moved (check
        the delivered padding). Sky != 128 -> Word is inflating or ignoring
        the row (C3 fails; check line-height/height in the delivered HTML).
        M1 taller than M3 -> the button outgrew the slot in that client.
    -->
    <mj-raw>
      <!-- en-tools-keep TPL General Email Template (__TEMPLATE_DATE__) v__TEMPLATE_VERSION__ -->
      <meta name="color-scheme" content="light dark" />
      <meta name="supported-color-schemes" content="light dark" />
      <meta name="format-detection" content="telephone=no" />
    </mj-raw>

    <!-- Baseline everything inherits -->
    <mj-attributes>
      <mj-text
        color="#000000"
        font-size="18px"
        font-weight="400"
        font-family="Tahoma, sans-serif"
        line-height="24px"
        css-class="wysiwyg"
      />
      <mj-button width="auto"
        css-class="button"
        font-size="18px"
        font-weight="700"
        font-family="Tahoma, sans-serif"
        line-height="22px"
        border-radius="100px"
        inner-padding="12px 16px"
        letter-spacing="1px"
        color="#ffffff"
        background-color="#006837"
      />
      <mj-image
        padding="0" />

      <!-- Reusable variants — apply with mj-class="*" -->
      <mj-class
        name="caption"
        font-size="10px"
        line-height="16px"
        padding="4px 0 0 0"
      />
    </mj-attributes>

    <!-- VIEWPORT FORK — lives HERE, not in styles.css (user decision
         2026-08-22). styles.css ships as the Template Styles BLOCK, in the
         body, so an email an editor assembled without adding that block has
         none of it. Most rules degrade gracefully when that happens; a fork
         does not. .mobile-only depends on a hide/reveal pair, and with the
         sheet absent NEITHER half applies — so both copies render and the
         reader sees the content twice. Rules whose absence reveals content
         meant to be hidden belong in the template head.

         data-en-tools-template-css marks it exempt from head extraction. It
         is deliberately NOT data-en-tools-band: that marker is subtracted
         before the Gmail budget is measured (check-catalog) and before the
         head-css hash (version-sync), because the band is builder chrome
         that never reaches an inbox. This CSS does reach the inbox and must
         keep counting.

         The pair is ATOMIC in this one block, and the reveal's @media
         condition is deliberately UNIQUE across every CSS source in the
         email — that uniqueness, not position, is what protects the order.
         The 2026-08-22 design assumed this head came FIRST in EN's merge so
         the reveal block would be the fold target; the 2026-08-24 final-QC
         delivered heads (EoA hOB4q6MD…/hb3nYGds…) measured the opposite:
         EN merges BODY style blocks (the Template Styles block carrying
         styles.css) AHEAD of the template head, so a reveal sharing
         styles.css's `(max-width: 599px)` string folded up past its own
         hide and the hide won everywhere below 599px. Latent only — no
         shipped block carries .mobile-only/.desktop-only today. The probe
         that blessed the old design (templateId 631) tested an email
         WITHOUT the styles block, which is exactly the case the fold cannot
         reach. `only screen and (max-width: 599px)` (with the space) is
         distinct from styles.css's `(max-width: 599px)`, from styles.css's
         `only screen and (max-width:599px)` (no space), and from MJML's
         emitted no-space block — nothing folds it, it stays after the 9998
         hide. Verify on the next send's delivered head: the reveal must
         appear AFTER the 9998 block.

         The hide is authored TWICE, on the .dark-only precedent, because the
         two copies serve different clients: the PLAIN copy is what EN inlines
         onto the element at send (!important stripped), covering clients that
         drop style blocks entirely — Gmail app on non-Google accounts, and
         most clients after a forward; the WRAPPED copy is an always-true
         media query EN returns verbatim (guide §2a), so the hide survives as
         a rule wherever the inline was lost. 9998px is deliberately distinct
         from the OWA block's 9999px so EN's same-condition merge cannot move
         it after the reveal.

         MEASURED 2026-08-24 (probe_vrect-and-template-head, EN templateId
         631, EoA XXhlSbq1e…): EN DOES inline a top-level rule from the
         template head exactly as it does the CSS Editor field — the
         delivered HTML carries display:none inline on the .mobile-only
         element, and this marker attribute survives delivery. The
         style-stripped audience is covered, and the fork rendered correctly
         on all 17 clients with NO styles.css in the email at all. -->
    <mj-raw>
      <style type="text/css" data-en-tools-template-css>
        /* HEAD-RESIDENT rules (catalogued 2026-08-24, user decision same day;
           see future-enhancements "Head-vs-block CSS"). Everything here must
           pass two tests: absence REVEALS or DUPLICATES content or breaks a
           mechanism every email relies on, and it would never be fixed by
           swapping the Template Styles block. Fix-by-block-swap rules stay in
           styles.css. EN inlines top-level rules from this head exactly as it
           does the CSS Editor field (measured 2026-08-24, EoA XXhlSbq1e…). */

        /* RTE margin containment — the bottom-only pacing model rests on
           these; without them every RTE block grows phantom margins. */
        /* Block elements only, enumerated (2026-09-25, EoA d0S0nH…): the old
           `*:first-child` / `*:last-child` also matched INLINE runs, so EN
           inlined margin-top/bottom:0 onto <strong>, <u>, <a>, <br>, and Word
           lets an inline run's margin zero its whole paragraph's spacing —
           adjacent formatted paragraphs ran together in Outlook (BugHerd 296).
           Enumerated rather than `div > *` because §2d bans child combinators. */
        .wysiwyg div p:first-child,
        .wysiwyg div h1:first-child,
        .wysiwyg div h2:first-child,
        .wysiwyg div h3:first-child,
        .wysiwyg div h4:first-child,
        .wysiwyg div h5:first-child,
        .wysiwyg div h6:first-child,
        .wysiwyg div ul:first-child,
        .wysiwyg div ol:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
        .wysiwyg div p:last-child,
        .wysiwyg div h1:last-child,
        .wysiwyg div h2:last-child,
        .wysiwyg div h3:last-child,
        .wysiwyg div h4:last-child,
        .wysiwyg div h5:last-child,
        .wysiwyg div h6:last-child,
        .wysiwyg div ul:last-child,
        .wysiwyg div ol:last-child { margin-bottom: 0; }

        /* Copy over a photo must be white even in an email built without the
           styles block (user decision 2026-08-24: relocated rather than
           re-authored inline). */
        .overlay h1, .overlay h2, .overlay h3, .overlay h4, .overlay h5,
        .overlay h6, .overlay span, .overlay p, .overlay a { color: #fff; }

        /* Viewport fork: hide twice, reveal once, in this order. */
        .mobile-only { display: none; }

        /* Mode fork (light/dark twins): the plain hide half. The dark twins
           sit in downlevel-revealed conditionals, so without this every
           non-Outlook client renders BOTH twins. Repaints stay in styles.css
           — only the swap mechanism lives here. */
        .dark-only { display: none; }
        :root { color-scheme: light dark; supported-color-schemes: light dark; }

        @media (max-width: 9998px) {
          .mobile-only { display: none !important; }
        }
        @media only screen and (max-width: 599px) {
          .desktop-only { display: none !important; }
          .mobile-only { display: block !important; }
        }
        /* Mode-fork reveals. These two blocks are the FIRST occurrence of
           their conditions, so styles.css's same-condition repaint blocks
           fold up to these positions at send — swap first, repaints after,
           which is byte-for-byte today's order inside styles.css. */
        @media only screen and (max-width: 9999px) {
          [data-ogsc] .dark-only { display: block !important; }
          [data-ogsc] .light-only { display: none !important; }
        }
        @media (prefers-color-scheme: dark) {
          .light-only { display: none !important; }
          .dark-only { display: block !important; }
        }
      </style>
    </mj-raw>
    <!-- Marketing Tools builder bands. The data-en-tools-band attribute keeps
         this sheet in the template HEAD: the importer splices every other
         style element out into the Template Styles block, and this layout has
         to survive an email built without that block. __TEMPLATE_VERSION__ is
         filled in at import from versions.json; keeping the literal here is
         what stops the version from feeding its own hash.

         TWO SEPARATE RULES, DELIBERATELY NOT MERGED INTO ONE SELECTOR LIST.

         BLOCK bands hang off a span the app injects into the block's own
         content, gated on whether a block wrapper exists anywhere in the
         document — true while editing in EN, false in a delivered email.

         The TEMPLATE band hangs off EN's container element instead. A span
         authored in the template shell does NOT work (measured 2026-08-20):
         EN's builder renders blocks inside the container and does not render
         the template's own surrounding markup, so such a span never enters
         the builder DOM at all. [data-container="main"] IS that container,
         and it is self-gating — the stored template carries EN's container
         placeholder there and a delivered email carries the blocks
         themselves, so nothing bears the attribute outside the editor.

         Do NOT write EN's container merge tag literally anywhere in this
         file. autoEnableTemplateReplacements splits the shell on the FIRST
         occurrence of that tag, so a literal in a comment splits the head at
         the comment instead of at the real container: the comment loses its
         terminator, swallows the whole stylesheet, and every light/dark image
         pair renders both halves. Measured 2026-08-20; check-catalog guards it.

         Merging the two selectors would be a live hazard: one unsupported
         selector in a group invalidates the WHOLE rule, so a client that
         cannot parse :has() would take the template band down with it. -->
    <mj-raw>
      <style type="text/css" data-en-tools-band>
        /* The TEMPLATE's own band leads, because it labels the whole email
           rather than a block inside it. Order is presentational only — the
           two selectors match different elements, so nothing in the cascade
           depends on it. */
        /* Builder canvas width. Only EN's editor ever renders an element
           bearing data-container (conventions, "the template band"), so this
           is chrome — here it works for every email, including one built
           without the styles block, and leaves the Gmail budget with the rest
           of the band (moved from styles.css 2026-08-24). */
        div[data-container] { max-width: 600px; margin: 0 auto; }
        [data-container="main"]:before{
          content: "Email Template v__TEMPLATE_VERSION__";
          display: block !important;
          font-weight: 700 !important;
          font-family: sans-serif !important;
          color: #4e535c !important;
          line-height: 1.75rem !important;
          text-align: center !important;
          margin-bottom: 2rem !important;
          background-color: #d0d5dd !important;
        }
        /* max-width tracks the EMAIL width — keep it equal to
           div[data-container]{max-width} in the band sheet, or the block band
           stops centring over the column it labels. check-catalog's
           "Builder band width tracks the email width" guard enforces it. */
        :is(body):has(.en__emailbuilder__block) .marketing-tools-banner{
          display: flex !important;
          font-weight: 700 !important;
          font-family: sans-serif !important;
          color: #4e535c !important;
          line-height: 2rem !important;
          top: 1.75rem !important;
          width: 100% !important;
          max-width: 600px !important;
          justify-content: center !important;
          background-color: #d0d5dd !important;
        }
        /* position:absolute belongs to the CSS Styles Block band ALONE (user
           decision 2026-08-21). That block's real content is a <style>, so it
           has no height of its own to sit above and the band has to be lifted
           out of flow; every other band labels a block with visible content,
           where taking the label out of flow makes it overlap what it labels.
           Targeted by id rather than a new class: each band span already
           carries a unique one — #head-styles and #raw-html, from
           STYLES_BAND_ID and RAW_HTML_BAND_ID — and those ids are baked into
           the stored content of blocks already living in EN, so minting new
           ones would desync every uploaded copy. Its own rule, never merged
           into the selector list above: one unparseable selector discards the
           WHOLE rule, and this gate is exactly the kind an older engine may
           not parse (conventions.md, "Builder bands"). */
        :is(body):has(.en__emailbuilder__block) #head-styles{
          position: absolute !important;
        }
      </style>
    </mj-raw>

    <mj-include path="../styles.css" type="css" />
  </mj-head>
  <mj-body css-class="email-body" background-color="#ffffff">
    <mj-raw><div style="color:#000000;background-color:#ffffff;margin:0;padding:0;"><div class="email-ground" style="background-color:#ffffff;margin:0;padding:0;"></mj-raw>

    <mj-section padding="16px 32px 8px"><mj-column><mj-text padding="0"><p style="font-size:14px;line-height:18px;color:#362229;margin:0;"><b>PROBE — Video overlay, 2026-09-29.</b> R = real band (eyeball). M = metered twins (count pixels against the Sun ruler). Every new band should be 338px.</p></mj-text></mj-column></mj-section>

    <!-- == R1 WATCH == -->
    <mj-section padding="16px 32px 8px"><mj-column><mj-text padding="0"><p style="font-size:14px;line-height:18px;color:#362229;margin:0;"><b>R1 WATCH</b>: option 1, as shipped. Expect 338px, button centred.</p></mj-text></mj-column></mj-section>
    <mj-wrapper background-color="#4A7A68" padding="0">
      <mj-section css-class="image-block block video-band" background-url="assets/photo-kayak_color-overlay.jpg" background-size="cover" background-repeat="no-repeat" background-position="center center" padding="105px 0 105px">
          <mj-group>
            <mj-column width="25px" vertical-align="middle">
              <mj-raw><tr><td height="128" style="height:128px;line-height:128px;font-size:0;">&nbsp;</td></tr></mj-raw>
            </mj-column>
            <mj-column width="550px" vertical-align="middle">
              <mj-button width="auto" href="https://tpl.org" align="center" background-color="#4A7A68" color="#ffffff" border="2px solid #ffffff" padding="0">WATCH&nbsp;&nbsp;▷</mj-button>
            </mj-column>
            <mj-column width="25px" vertical-align="middle">
              <mj-raw><tr><td height="128" style="height:128px;line-height:128px;font-size:0;">&nbsp;</td></tr></mj-raw>
            </mj-column>
          </mj-group>
      </mj-section>
    </mj-wrapper>

    <!-- == R2 PLAY ICON == -->
    <mj-section padding="16px 32px 8px"><mj-column><mj-text padding="0"><p style="font-size:14px;line-height:18px;color:#362229;margin:0;"><b>R2 PLAY ICON</b>: option 2. Expect 338px, 128px icon centred.</p></mj-text></mj-column></mj-section>
    <mj-wrapper background-color="#4A7A68" padding="0">
      <mj-section css-class="image-block block video-band" background-url="assets/photo-kayak_color-overlay.jpg" background-size="cover" background-repeat="no-repeat" background-position="center center" padding="105px 0 105px">
          <mj-group>
            <mj-column width="25px" vertical-align="middle">
              <mj-raw><tr><td height="128" style="height:128px;line-height:128px;font-size:0;">&nbsp;</td></tr></mj-raw>
            </mj-column>
            <mj-column width="550px" vertical-align="middle">
              <mj-image src="assets/icon-play-circle_orange.png" width="128px" alt="Play video" href="https://tpl.org" align="center" padding="0" />
            </mj-column>
            <mj-column width="25px" vertical-align="middle">
              <mj-raw><tr><td height="128" style="height:128px;line-height:128px;font-size:0;">&nbsp;</td></tr></mj-raw>
            </mj-column>
          </mj-group>
      </mj-section>
    </mj-wrapper>

    <!-- == R3 NONE == -->
    <mj-section padding="16px 32px 8px"><mj-column><mj-text padding="0"><p style="font-size:14px;line-height:18px;color:#362229;margin:0;"><b>R3 NONE</b>: option 3. Expect 338px, photo only.</p></mj-text></mj-column></mj-section>
    <mj-wrapper background-color="#4A7A68" padding="0">
      <mj-section css-class="image-block block video-band" background-url="assets/photo-kayak_color-overlay.jpg" background-size="cover" background-repeat="no-repeat" background-position="center center" padding="105px 0 105px">
          <mj-group>
            <mj-column width="25px" vertical-align="middle">
              <mj-raw><tr><td height="128" style="height:128px;line-height:128px;font-size:0;">&nbsp;</td></tr></mj-raw>
            </mj-column>
            <mj-column width="550px" vertical-align="middle">
              <mj-raw><!-- Row 1 Button - Not Displayed --></mj-raw>
